00:29:20.000 --> 00:30:21.000
Because even though you may have been their age, you were their age under very different circumstances than your child is living with now. And you remember earlier I said that there's about a five-year difference in how long it takes to make the full transition into adulthood for when we compare the current generation with the previous generation. And so my advice to parents who can't resist thinking when I was your age is don't compare your child at the age of 30 with where you were when you were 30. subtracts five years from your life when you're making the comparison. So let's say if your child is 35 now, compare how your child is doing to how you were doing when you were 30. That's subtracting the five years to make the comparison more accurate. And you may find actually that even though you had thought that your child wasn't moving fast
因为尽管你当时可能和他们一样大，但你生活的环境与你孩子现在所处的环境有着极大的不同。你还记得我之前说过，与上一代相比，现在这一代人完成向成年期的全面过渡大约需要晚五年左右。因此，我对那些忍不住心里想着“当年我像你这么大”的父母的建议是：不要把你30岁的孩子与你30岁时的状态进行比较。在进行比较时，从你的人生中减去五岁。所以，假设你的孩子现在35岁，请把你的孩子现在的表现与你30岁时的表现进行比较。通过减去这五年，让比较更加准确。你会发现，虽然你之前可能觉得你的孩子没有取得足够的进展，

38
00:30:21.000 --> 00:31:01.000
enough that in fact they're quite accomplished relative to where you were at the generationally appropriate comparison point. Now, what happens when adult children get married or have children of their own? How do those new relationships tend to affect the parent-child relationship? And what can parents and children do to keep their relationship strong through those transitions? Well, I think that – let's talk first about marriage and how your child's marriage. may affect the relationship that you have with them. You have this new person in your life, and you didn't choose this person, and you didn't raise this person, and that's your child's partner.
但事实上，按照代际适当的比较点来看，他们相对于你当年已经相当有成就了。那么，当成年子女结婚或有了自己的孩子时，这些新关系往往会如何影响亲子关系？为了让亲子关系在这些转变中保持牢固，父母和孩子又能做些什么呢？嗯，我认为——我们先来谈谈婚姻，以及你孩子的婚姻可能会如何影响你与他们的关系。你的生活中多了一个新成员，而这个人不是你选的，也不是你养大的，这是你孩子的伴侣。

39
00:31:01.000 --> 00:32:09.000
And you're going to have to find a way to get along with this person in order to keep your relationship with your child strong and healthy. And I think that if you're blessed, as I am, I have a daughter-in-law whom I'm quite close to and like very much, then that's terrific. That makes life a lot easier for you. If, on the other hand, you're not so crazy about this person, you can have a cordial relationship with them and avoid having conflict arise. Because if conflict arises between you and your child's partner, it is going to affect the quality of the relationship that you have with your child. Let's shift for a second and talk about when your child becomes a parent and you become a grandparent. and you're watching your child's parenting behavior, and you're not wild about the way that they're parenting their child. What I say in the book is to remember that child-bearing advice, you know, it changes with fads and fashions.
你必须找到一种与这个人相处的方法，才能保持你与孩子之间稳固健康的亲子关系。我认为，如果你有福气（就像我一样），我有一个关系相当亲密且非常喜欢的儿媳，那真是太棒了。这会让你的生活轻松许多。另一方面，如果你对这个人没那么感冒，你可以与他们保持一种客套的关系，避免冲突的发生。因为如果在你和孩子的伴侣之间产生冲突，势必会影响你与孩子关系的质量。我们花一秒钟转换一下话题，来谈谈当你的孩子成为父母、你成为祖父母时，当你看着你孩子的育儿行为，却对他们的带娃方式不怎么感冒时。我在书中所说的是，要记住育儿建议这东西，它总是随着一时的流行趋势而改变。

40
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:49.000
And the advice that young people who are new parents today are getting from advice books, from their pediatricians, from their friends, is very different than the advice that their parents got. when they were new parents. And so you may see your child doing something that stuns you because it's not the way that you raised them. And you may be tempted to comment on it. And I think you should just take a breath and remember that they probably have gotten very different advice about how to parent than you got when you were a new parent. And what I hear is that today's new parents follow a very strict and rigid sketching
现在的新手父母从育儿书籍、儿科医生和朋友那里得到的建议，与他们的父母当年做新手父母时得到的建议大不相同。因此，你可能会看到你的孩子做一些让你震惊的事，因为那不是你抚养他们的方式。你可能会忍不住想评头论足一番。我认为你应该深呼吸一下，记住他们得到的关于如何育儿的建议，可能和你当年当新手父母时截然不同。我听到的是，如今的新手父母遵循着非常严格和刻板的计划

41
00:32:49.000 --> 00:33:38.000
for, let's say, feeding and sleeping than their parents did, who were much more relaxed. I note in the book that my generation of parents followed Dr. Spock's advice. The first sentence in his book is, Trust Yourself. If that book were written today, the first sentence would be trust the data, because new parents today entered data into their smartphones on their children's eating, how many ounces of breast milk or formula they've taken at each point during the day, how many minutes of sleep they've gotten for each time they've put the baby down. In my generation, you know, you look for signs that your child was hungry and you fed them.
比如在喂养和睡眠方面，就比他们的父母要严格得多，他们的父母要随性得多。我在书中指出，我这一代父母遵循的是斯波克博士的建议。他书中的第一句话是：相信你自己。如果这本书是今天写的，第一句话就会变成相信数据，因为如今的新手父母会将关于孩子饮食的数据输入智能手机，记录一天中每个时间段吃了多少盎司母乳或配方奶，以及每次哄睡后睡了多少分钟。而在我们这一代，你知道的，你只要观察孩子饿了的迹象，然后喂他们就行了。

42
00:33:38.000 --> 00:34:30.000
You look for signs that they were full and you stopped feeding them. You look for signs that they were sleeping and you put them down for a nap and you let them sleep as long as they would sleep. that's not the way it's done today. And so I think many parents in my generation are quite surprised at the way their children are parenting. But that doesn't mean that their children are wrong because, frankly, it probably doesn't matter for the child's development, whether they're on a tight schedule or not. You need to remember as a grandparent that your child, who's now a parent, perhaps for the first time, is often insecure and anxious and nervous. about being a parent. They haven't done it before. And the best thing that you can do as a grandparent is to make your child feel comfortable and confident. And criticizing their parenting is not going to do that.
你观察他们吃饱的迹象，然后就不喂了。你观察他们有睡意迹象，然后把他们放下去小睡，让他们想睡多久就睡多久。但现在不这么做了。所以我想我这一代许多父母对他们孩子的育儿方式感到相当惊讶。但这并不意味着他们的孩子做错了，因为坦率地说，对于孩子的发展来说，是否按照紧凑的时间表生活可能根本不重要。作为祖父母，你需要记住的是，你的孩子现在自己也成了父母，也许还是头一次当父母，他们常常对当父母感到没有安全感、焦虑和紧张。他们以前没做过。作为祖父母，你能做的最好的事就是让你的孩子感到舒适和自信。批评他们的育儿方式可起不到这个作用。

43
00:34:30.000 --> 00:35:27.000
Last question. Is there one piece of advice you would like to give our listeners today, or maybe two pieces, one for the parents of adult children and one for listeners who are themselves adult children? Yeah, I guess it's the same advice for both. It's to be compassionate and to try to look at the situation from the other generation's perspective. I'll tell one brief anecdote if I have time. When I was recording the audiobook of you and your adult child, I was working with two young people in their late 20s, the producer and the audio engineer. And they had to listen very carefully as I narrated the book in order to stop me every once in a while and say, could you do that sentence over again? It didn't sound right. There was background noise. And for each of these young people, this was their first exposure to the book. Neither had read the book before.
最后一个问题。今天有没有什么建议想送给我们的听众，或者也许是两个建议，一个给成年子女的父母，另一个给身兼成年子女身份的听众？是的，我想这两个群体得到的建议是一样的。那就是要富有同情心，试着从另一代人的角度来看待局势。如果时间允许的话，我讲一个简短的轶事。当我在录制《你与成年的孩子》的有声书时，我正与两个二十多岁年轻人合作，分别是制作人和音频工程师。他们必须非常仔细地听我朗读这本书，以便每隔一段时间就能打断我说，那句话能重录一遍吗？听起来不对，有背景噪声。对于这两个年轻人来说，这是他们第一次接触这本书。两人以前都没读过这本书。

44
00:35:27.000 --> 00:36:20.000
But they had to listen very carefully to my narration of it. And after the first day, the young woman who was the producer took me aside and she said, my parents have to read this book because they don't understand. understand me. And three days later, the audio engineer took me aside and said, I got to get a copy of this book from my parents because they don't understand what it's like to be my age these days. But I think there's also a need for the young adult, the grown child, to be compassionate toward their parents and to understand that this is not such an easy situation for parents to be in either. So it's a challenge. I think most families can get through it. But I think Being understanding of what the other person is experiencing at this time is going to go a long way toward helping to strengthen the relationship between parents and their adult children.
但他们必须非常仔细地聆听我的旁白。第一天结束后，身为制作人的年轻女性把我拉到一边对我说，我父母得读读这本书，因为他们不理解我。三天后，音频工程师把我拉到一边说，我得从我父母那里弄一本这本书，因为他们不理解如今像我这个年纪是什么样的。但我认为，年轻的成年人、成年的孩子同样需要对父母抱有同情心，去理解父母目前也处于一个相当不易的处境中。所以这是一个挑战。我认为大多数家庭都能挺过去。但我认为，理解对方在这个时期所经历的一切，对于增进父母与成年子女之间的关系将大有裨益。

45
